RUNTIME AND BUILD DEPENDENCIES
==============================

The following is an inventory of dependency boundaries visible in source,
not a resolved bill of materials. Build arguments and unpinned dependencies
can change the contents of an image. Preserve each component's own license,
copyright, attribution, and source-distribution obligations where applicable.

* app/cetirun-chart/Dockerfile.base installs Azure Linux system packages,
  Node.js/npm, GitHub CLI, kubectl, Azure CLI, ripgrep, supervisor, ttyd,
  Tailscale, Oh My Zsh, Codex CLI, OpenCode, uv/uvx, Claude Code, and their
  dependencies. Source-built tools copied as binaries still need their
  applicable notices; a binary COPY is not evidence that those notices ship.
  Commercial/service terms for installed tools are not granted by CetiRun's MIT
  license.
* agents/openclaw/Dockerfile adds OpenClaw, ClawHub, the OpenClaw ACP, Brave,
  Discord, Feishu, Perplexity, and Slack extensions.
* agents/hermes/Dockerfile installs the checksum-pinned Hermes Agent
  v2026.9.24 source release, its checked-in uv/npm dependency locks, and the
  upstream-pinned SQLite compatibility library.
* Dockerfile.desktop builds or installs Xorg/Xvfb, Fluxbox, LibVNCServer,
  x11vnc, supervisor, websockify, noVNC, Noto CJK fonts, Google Chrome, and
  system libraries. This is not an MIT-only software stack. In particular,
  x11vnc includes GPLv2 terms and noVNC contains MPL 2.0 core code plus
  differently licensed web assets. Google Chrome is distributed under its own
  terms. Review corresponding-source and notice requirements for the actual
  desktop image, including source modifications made by the Dockerfile.
* app/cetirun-ai-gateway/Dockerfile installs LiteLLM (MIT) and the Python
  packages pinned in app/cetirun-ai-gateway/requirements.txt, Node.js, and
  the Prisma CLI and engines fetched at build time. It deliberately omits
  litellm-enterprise, which carries the BerriAI Enterprise License. The
  bundled PostgreSQL and Valkey run their own Azure Linux images from MCR.
* app/cetirun-observability/Dockerfile builds the ADX-Mon collector and
  ingestor (MIT, Copyright (c) Microsoft Corporation) from a checksum-pinned
  upstream commit with the Go modules vendored in that source, and ships its
  license as /licenses/adx-mon.LICENSE. The cetirun-observability chart also
  runs kube-state-metrics (Apache-2.0) from its MCR image, pinned by digest,
  and installs the ADX-Mon custom resource definitions from the same
  upstream commit.
* The portal binary embeds a frontend bundle built from
  app/cetirun-portal/web/package-lock.json. Its generated JavaScript notice
  accompanies that bundle and includes installed production dependencies and
  upstream license files. This conservative inventory can include packages
  removed by tree shaking. Go dependencies, container packages, and assets
  copied outside that dependency graph still need separate review.
* The operator and portal link Go dependencies identified by their respective
  go.mod/go.sum files, including Kubernetes, controller-runtime, Helm, and
  Azure SDK packages as applicable. Their runtime images also contain the
  selected base image's packages.
* The infrastructure/chart configuration can deploy OAuth2 Proxy and use
  Istio, monitoring agents, storage drivers, and user-selected images or skill
  packages. These are independent distributions, not relicensed CetiRun source.

Desktop license reference examples:
https://github.com/LibVNC/x11vnc/blob/0.9.16/COPYING
https://github.com/novnc/noVNC/blob/v1.5.0/LICENSE.txt

